anking singles, but wastes powerful 2s early.\n\nBetter: start with **triple_with_single** or **straight** to maintain flexibility.\n\nThe longest straight: ♠4 ♠5 ♠6 ♦7 ♠8 ♠9 ♣10 ♠J ♣Q ♥K ♣A — 11 cards! This is enormous progress, reducing my hand drastically.\n\nWait — is this straight valid? Let’s check:  \nRanks: 4,5,6,7,8,9,10,J,Q,K,A — yes, 11 consecutive ranks from 4 to A. No 2s or jokers, so allowed. And I have all these cards.\n\nThis single move removes 11 of my 19 cards — over half my hand — and puts immense pressure on the peasants. It\'s unlikely they can beat an 11-card straight, and even if they bomb, I retain strong cards (♥2, ♦2, BlackJoker, remaining Qs).\n\nAlternatively, playing the triple 4s with a high attached pair like AA or 22 could be safe, but less aggressive.\n\nBest choice: play the **longest possible straight** to maximize card reduction and tempo.\n\nFrom the valid moves list:\nstraight: ♠4 ♠5 ♠6 ♦7 ♠8 ♠9 ♣10 ♠J ♣Q ♥K ♣A → this is listed and valid.\n\nYes — it appears in the list.\n\nReason: Playing this 11-card straight dramatically reduces my hand, applies immediate pressure, and is very difficult for opponents to beat without a bomb or rocket. Given that I have high cards and bombs (triple Qs, potential for future bombs) and the Black Joker, I can recover control later. As landlord, fast card elimination is prioritized.\n\nAnswer: {"cards": ["♠4", "♠5", "♠6", "♦7", "♠8", "♠9", "♣10", "♠J", "♣Q", "♥K", "♣A"]}'}
